#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535320 :question/problem "![](images/66/66c8e80f89c7964cae5443f9e3cb04916ec6aee3e16f27480d8d85d3a344187e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta \\left((r\\cos\\theta)^{2} - (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\cos(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535330 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535332 :question/problem "![](images/66/66c8e80f89c7964cae5443f9e3cb04916ec6aee3e16f27480d8d85d3a344187e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta 2 (r\\cos\\theta) (r\\sin\\theta) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535337 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4a332a1bea58e47ab58fa47c6944213ce4caa5d26e6bfb129fea613adcd524b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta \\left((r\\cos\\theta)^{2} - (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\cos(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535342 :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535347 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535354 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(6\\sqrt{2},{45}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535355 :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535357 :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(9,{300}^{∘}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =9\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535370 :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(7,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =7\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{7}{2},-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535373 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(3\\sqrt{3},{240}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{9}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535374 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,{315}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =2\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535386 :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535387 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},{120}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535395 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(3,{120}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535415 :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=8{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 8{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-16z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =16z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535416 :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=7{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 7{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(7{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-14z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =14z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535937 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,3)$ to the point $N(3,-1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(3\\,i-j-(i+3\\,j)) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3-4t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=3-4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+1-(3-4t)}(2+(-4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t-2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6t-2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{6(1)-2}-{e}^{6(0)-2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{6}}{3{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536002 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-(i+j)) \\\\  & =i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{(2t+1)^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-\\frac{1}{2t+1}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 1+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+1}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 0+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 0+1}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536059 :question/problem "![](images/4e/4efe76bd06d22c5735b427c150f5a09b7288f8281b90921aeb11902c864176e5.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at the origin and has semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{9x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536061 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(3 x^{2} + x - 1)\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e^{2}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3 x^{2} + x - 1$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=6 x + 1,\\quad v'(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(3 x^{2} + x - 1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (6 x + 1) \\\\ &=\\frac{6 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 x^{2} + x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + x - 1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e^{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=e^{2}} &=(3 (e^{2})^{2} + (e^{2}) - 1)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{(e^{2})}\\right)+(\\ln{\\left((e^{2}) \\right)})\\cdot (6 (e^{2}) + 1) \\\\ &=(-1 + e^{2} + 3 e^{4})\\cdot (e^{-2})+(2)\\cdot (1 + 6 e^{2}) \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{e^{2}} + 3 + 15 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- \\frac{1}{e^{2}} + 3 + 15 e^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536065 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(4 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=4 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=4 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(4 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 1)\\cdot (4 e^{x}) \\\\ &=4 \\left(x + 2\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(4 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 1)\\cdot (4 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(4)\\cdot (1)+(1)\\cdot (4) \\\\ &=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536069 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - x - 1)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - x - 1$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 1,\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - x - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 1) \\\\ &=- x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} &=(- \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)^{2} - \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})+(\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - 1) \\\\ &=(-1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\frac{\\pi}{2} - 1) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(- 12 \\pi - 32 - \\pi^{2}\\right)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(- 12 \\pi - 32 - \\pi^{2}\\right)}{32}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536071 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- e^{x})\\cdot (x + 3)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 3)\\cdot (- e^{x}) \\\\ &=- \\left(x + 4\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(- e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 3)\\cdot (- e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(-1)\\cdot (1)+(3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ &=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536074 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- 4 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- 4 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 4 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- 4 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 1)\\cdot (- 4 e^{x}) \\\\ &=- 4 \\left(x + 2\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(- 4 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 1)\\cdot (- 4 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(-4)\\cdot (1)+(1)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ &=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536075 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - 2 x + 2)\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e^{2}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - 2 x + 2$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 2,\\quad v'(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - 2 x + 2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 2) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + x^{2} + 2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 x - 2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e^{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=e^{2}} &=(- (e^{2})^{2} - 2 (e^{2}) + 2)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{(e^{2})}\\right)+(\\ln{\\left((e^{2}) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 (e^{2}) - 2) \\\\ &=(- e^{4} - 2 e^{2} + 2)\\cdot (e^{-2})+(2)\\cdot (- 2 e^{2} - 2) \\\\ &=- 5 e^{2} - 6 + \\frac{2}{e^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- 5 e^{2} - 6 + \\frac{2}{e^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536077 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- 3 x^{2} + 3 x - 4)\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- 3 x^{2} + 3 x - 4$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=3 - 6 x,\\quad v'(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- 3 x^{2} + 3 x - 4)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (3 - 6 x) \\\\ &=- \\frac{6 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 x^{2} - 3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 x + 4}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=e} &=(- 3 (e)^{2} + 3 (e) - 4)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{(e)}\\right)+(\\ln{\\left((e) \\right)})\\cdot (3 - 6 (e)) \\\\ &=(- 3 e^{2} - 4 + 3 e)\\cdot (e^{-1})+(1)\\cdot (3 - 6 e) \\\\ &=- 9 e - \\frac{4}{e} + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- 9 e - \\frac{4}{e} + 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536087 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - 3 x - 4)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - 3 x - 4$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 3,\\quad v'(x)=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - 3 x - 4)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 3) \\\\ &=x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}} &=(- \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) - 4)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})+(\\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) - 3) \\\\ &=(- \\frac{9 \\pi}{4} - \\frac{9 \\pi^{2}}{16} - 4)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} - 3) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(9 \\pi^{2} + 112 + 60 \\pi\\right)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(9 \\pi^{2} + 112 + 60 \\pi\\right)}{32}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536090 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(3 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 2)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=3 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(3 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 2)\\cdot (3 e^{x}) \\\\ &=3 \\left(x + 3\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(3 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 2)\\cdot (3 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(3)\\cdot (1)+(2)\\cdot (3) \\\\ &=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536098 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- 3 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- 3 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 3 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- 3 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 1)\\cdot (- 3 e^{x}) \\\\ &=- 3 \\left(x + 2\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(- 3 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 1)\\cdot (- 3 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(-3)\\cdot (1)+(1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536110 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{2t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}+\\frac{16{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2t}(2\\sin^{2}⁡4t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536116 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{3t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{3t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2({e}^{3t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j)\\cdot (3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3t}\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}(3\\sin^{2}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536131 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{4t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{4t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{4t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}{1+4({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{e}^{4t}\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,j)\\cdot (4{e}^{4t}\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{4t}\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}+\\frac{12{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{4t}(4\\sin^{2}⁡3t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536134 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + y^{2})\\,i+2 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=3 e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 e^{t})^{2})\\,i+2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(3 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+3 e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+3 e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(3 e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{9 e^{2 t}}{t}+18 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =18 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{9 e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536143 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 3 x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 3 x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 3 x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + 3 y^{2})\\,i+6 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=2 e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+3(2 e^{t})^{2})\\,i+6(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+12 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((12 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+12 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((12 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(12 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{12 e^{2 t}}{t}+24 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =24 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{12 e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536144 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + y^{2})\\,i+2 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+(e^{t})^{2})\\,i+2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{e^{2 t}}{t}+2 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =2 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536146 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{4t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{8xy}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{4t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}}{1+16({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{8({e}^{4t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)}{1+16({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sin^{2}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,i+\\frac{8{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{e}^{4t}\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4\\sin^{2}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,i+\\frac{8{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,j)\\cdot (4{e}^{4t}\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{16{e}^{4t}\\sin^{2}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}+\\frac{40{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{4t}(4\\sin^{2}⁡5t+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536147 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x + 2 y^{2})\\,i+4 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=2 e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+2(2 e^{t})^{2})\\,i+4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(8 e^{2 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+8 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((8 e^{2 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+8 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((8 e^{2 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(8 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{8 e^{2 t}}{t}+16 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =16 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{8 e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536163 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{3t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{3t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{3t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{1+4({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j)\\cdot (3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{3t}\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}+\\frac{8{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{3t}(3\\sin^{2}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536169 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+e^{2 t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + 2 y^{2})\\,i+4 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=e^{2 t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+2(e^{2 t})^{2})\\,i+4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(e^{2 t})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 e^{4 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{2 t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2 e^{4 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((2 e^{4 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{2 t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{2 e^{4 t}}{t}+8 e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =8 e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{2 e^{4 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
